= Zero-knowledge proof — Technical =

A proof system for relation R aims for completeness and soundness; zero knowledge is formalized by the existence of a simulator whose output is indistinguishable, under the chosen definition, from a verifier's view of real proofs. Concrete systems vary in setup, succinctness, prover/verifier cost, and cryptographic assumptions.

== Modeling / security cautions ==
* zero-knowledge proof ≠ encryption
* zero knowledge ≠ application-level anonymity
* proof of statement ≠ proof that surrounding data/source is trustworthy

